Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Stopwatches Chewing Sand
- Story & Context: When a power surge melted an E-mu sampler's memory bank at the Bir Qena depot, Tariq Vance averted an armed riot between rival scrap crews by wiring the surviving drum unit directly into an expat's overdriven Marshall stack. The room was choked with the acrid smell of frying solder and cardamom tea as the busted box kicked into a punishing, swung 16th-note groove that stopped the brawl dead in its tracks. Grabbing a dented talkback mic from the rubble, Spanner sneered into the feedback, shouting, "Look at your wrist, mate, the second hand is choking on the dunes!" The improvised clash became Solder-Strut's opening manifesto, recorded onto a heat-warped cassette before the generator died.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An oxidized digital stopwatch with a shattered LCD display half-buried in cracked desert mud, surrounded by loose spools of magnetic audio tape and brass synthesizer keys glowing under a harsh midday haze.

Track 2: "Who Kept the Tuesday Ledger?"
- Story & Context: Huddled behind the windbreak of the Oasis 44 pumping station, Spanner tracked this bitter confession after abandoning his logistical shift to barter for a crate of contraband Roland ROM cards. With cold water condensation dripping off a stolen tin of lager directly onto the fader board, he laid down jagged acoustic guitar strums over a syncopated, sync-locked synth-brass fanfare. The song recounts the missed border checkpoint that permanently stranded his convoy partner in the north, punctuated by the mournful, terrace-chant hook: "I gave away our Sunday ticket just to hear the solder pop." The track quickly circulated among desert haulers as an unvarnished anthem of gear-obsession and betrayal.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A wind-whipped ledger book with scorched calendar pages fluttering open on an oil-stained flight case, lit by the green diagnostic glow of a rackmount synthesizer in an arid wasteland night.

Track 3: Gated Reverb for the Eleventh Hour
- Story & Context: In the salt flats of Wadi Natrun, myth surrounds a decommissioned mobile pirate radio flatbed known as The Juggernaut, which supposedly broke an axle during a sandstorm while its sequencers were still running on solar power. Nomad traders claim the abandoned rig's automated clock circuit still triggers every afternoon at precisely 11:00 AM, firing off colossal, gated snare snaps and blaring synthetic horns across the barren scrub. A vocal track cut into the ROM chip loops endlessly against the dry gale, taunting listeners with the defiant line: "You're five years late for the soundcheck, but the groove won't pay your bail." The song exists as the scene’s legendary ghost transmission, played by every caravan crew whenever their schedules slip past redemption.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A rusted six-wheeled flatbed truck draped in sun-bleached solar panels on an endless salt pan, horn speakers mounted on its roof vibrating dust off the metal in the blinding desert heat.

